  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 323 PM MDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nHigher probabilities of afternoon showers and thunderstorms will \ncharacterize the first portion of the long-term forecast period as \nan enhanced push of monsoonal moisture moves through southeast \nWyoming and western Nebraska. Both ECMWF ensemble and GEFS ensemble \nguidance show PWAT normalized anomalies peaking near the 2 standard-\ndeviations threshold through Wednesday afternoon. PWAT values \nbetween 1 inch and 1.25 inches are seen in forecast soundings \nduring the afternoon hours. Additionally, a bit of enhanced \nwesterly flow aloft in the 500-200mb layer will round the apex \nof the monsoonal ridge over the southwestern CONUS. This \nenhanced flow aloft, while certainly not extreme, will be just \nenough to add a bit higher bulk shear values to the convective \nsetup. Convection will likely develop over the higher terrain \nof southeast Wyoming and drift east-southeastward by 18-20z. As\noutflow boundaries pool, expect a chance of isolated gusty \nwinds perhaps occasionally to severe limits of 50 knots in the \neastern high plains of Wyoming and western Nebraska. While \ntemperatures will run slightly cooler the enhanced monsoonal \nmoisture, deeply mixed afternoon forecast soundings continue to \nsupport a gusty wind threat. Thunderstorms should have enough \nforward momentum to limit the risk of flash flooding. However, \nany location that does see repeated rounds of showers and \nthunderstorms could experience brief flash flooding concerns.\n\nBy Thursday, the monsoon high pressure over the southwest becomes a \nbit more suppressed and the plume of higher moisture values drifts \neastward away from the Rocky Mountain region. As a result, expect \nlower chances of afternoon showers and storms from Thursday through \nnext weekend. High temperatures will rebound somewhat as we dry out, \nbut some uncertainty in the temperature and precipitation forecast \nemerges by the weekend. A frontal boundary from the northern high \nplains attempts to drop southward into the eastern plains of Wyoming \nand western Nebraska perhaps as early as Friday afternoon. Ensemble \nguidance varies greatly with regard to any impacts...ranging from \nonly slightly cooler temperatures to a frontogenetically forced band \nof showers and cloud cover over the high plains on Friday evening, \nor perhaps on Saturday. With such low confidence from Day 5 onward \nin the forecast, we will message a more conservative outcome for now \nwith slightly less hot weather and isolated shower chances. \nRegardless, significant precipitation seems unlikely through the end \nof the long term with generally above-average temperatures \ncontinuing for the foreseeable future despite a few frontal \nboundaries nearby.",
